/-! Guidance from the project lead (the requester):

# Zassenhaus / Frobenius (lead's design; certain classical lemmas, used by GAP-A for all n and by the Kazhdan–Margulis chapter)
Setting: conjugate z (resp. p) to 0 by a boost B_z (HypIsometryProp/boost lemmas in the workspace). For g near identity at 0
use the O⁺-lift L(g) (tsign • mat of the representative) and the Euclidean operator norm; NearIdAt g 0 η ⇔ ‖L g − 1‖ small
(two-sided: prove `nearId_iff_norm`: NearIdAt g 0 η → ‖L g − 1‖ ≤ κ(η), and ‖L g − 1‖ ≤ κ' → NearIdAt g 0 (c·κ'), κ, c explicit).
## zassenhausNilpotent (Raghunathan, Discrete subgroups of Lie groups, Lemma 8.16 / Thm 8.16)
1. Commutator contraction: ‖[A,C] − 1‖ ≤ 2‖A−1‖‖C−1‖‖A⁻¹‖‖C⁻¹‖ ≤ 4‖A−1‖‖C−1‖ when ‖A−1‖,‖C−1‖ ≤ 1/4.
   (Bieberbach's version mOf_commutator in bieberbach.lean is the same computation.)
2. With U := {‖L g − 1‖ < 1/8}: every simple commutator [s₁,[s₂,…,s_k]] of elements of U has norm ≤ 2^{−k}·(1/8)·8 → 0.
3. Γ discrete ⇒ Γ ∩ {‖L g − 1‖ ≤ 1/8} is finite ⇒ min{‖L g − 1‖ : g ∈ Γ∖1, ‖L g − 1‖ ≤ 1/8} = m > 0 ⇒ all simple
   commutators of weight ≥ N (2^{−N} < m) of elements of S := Γ ∩ U vanish.
4. Group theory: if all simple commutators of weight N in S ∪ S⁻¹ vanish then ⟨S⟩ is nilpotent (γ_N⟨S⟩ is generated by
   simple commutators of weight ≥ N in S ∪ S⁻¹ — Hall; check Mathlib `lowerCentralSeries`, `Subgroup.closure_commutator`;
   S is closed under inverses here). Choose η with NearIdAt γ z η ⇒ B_z⁻¹γB_z ∈ U.
## frobeniusCommute (Frobenius; key step of Jordan's theorem)
H finite, fixes p (conjugate p = 0 ⇒ H ⊂ O(n) block). For a, c ∈ H near identity: c₀ := c, c_{k+1} := [a, c_k];
‖c_{k+1} − 1‖ ≤ 4‖a − 1‖‖c_k − 1‖ ≤ ½‖c_k − 1‖ ⇒ c_k → 1 ⇒ (H finite, the nonzero norms have a positive minimum) c_N = 1.
Frobenius lemma: for unitary/orthogonal A, B with ‖A − 1‖ < 1/2 (say) and A commuting with [A,B]: A and B commute.
(Proof: [A,B] = A·(BA⁻¹B⁻¹) commutes with A ⇒ A and BAB⁻¹ commute… standard: the eigenvalues of A and BAB⁻¹ coincide and all
lie in an arc of length < π/3; commuting ⇒ simultaneously diagonalisable ⇒ A⁻¹·BAB⁻¹ has eigenvalues ratio of eigenvalues
in a small arc; and A⁻¹BAB⁻¹ = [A⁻¹,B] ∈ …; conclude BAB⁻¹ = A by the "unique eigenvalue matching" argument —
Curtis–Reiner §36, or Raghunathan Lemma 8.18.) Backward induction from c_N = 1 gives [a, c] = 1.
-/

open scoped Matrix.Norms.Operator
